version: 3
metadata:
  name: basic_nav_rst
  title: Basic nav2 navigation on the roqsim/roqsim (MuJoCo) TurtleBot 4 (Depot)
  description: |
    A TurtleBot 4 navigates the nav2 Depot world to a per-configuration goal with nav2 + AMCL, in
    roqsim (MuJoCo). The roqsim half of the basic_nav pair: same nav2 params, same map and the same
    measurement half as basic_nav_gazebo.vast -- only the simulator differs. The bridge publishes
    the same ROS graph Gazebo does (incl. a ground-truth turtlebot4_base_link_gt frame), so
    roqsim is a drop-in replacement.

    Three containers, which is what this file is really about. `simulation` is roqsim, started by
    the backend from its own image -- nothing here installs a simulator. `sut` is a nav2 image kept
    vanilla: RoboVAST layers scenario_execution_server onto it and the scenario drives its launch
    over remote("ipc:///ipc/sut"). `scenario` is the stock RoboVAST image, so this campaign builds
    no image of its own.
  keywords: [robotics, navigation, ROS2, mujoco, roqsim, nav2, depot]
configuration:
- name: goal
  variations:
  # Map-frame goals; the robot starts at the map origin. Identical to basic_nav_gazebo.vast.
  - ParameterVariationList:
      scenario: goal_pose
      values:
      - position: {x: 21.0, y: 6.0}
      - position: {x: 21.0, y: 4.0}
      - position: {x: 21.0, y: -2.0}
      - position: {x: 21.0, y: -4.0}
      - position: {x: 21.0, y: -6.0}
visualization:
  # The Config tab's third column: what each generated configuration contains, beside the editor.
  config:
    panels:
    - parameters: {title: Scenario parameters, height: "35%"}
    - world: {title: World configuration, height: "15%"}
    # The grid this campaign navigates, with this configuration's endpoints drawn on it. `map2d` and
    # not `scene3d`: the 3D panel keys its geometry on the simulator image, and an experiment .vast
    # names none -- the deployment's project resolves it, per campaign, after apply_backend. The
    # config view has no campaign, so it asks the file and is refused for want of an image; there
    # would be nothing to look at. The 2D map needs nothing but the two files below, and it is the
    # view nav2 plans in anyway.
    - map2d:
        title: Map
        # The very map the scenario hands nav2 (scenario_roqsim.osc: key_value('map',
        # '/config/files/depot.yaml')), so this is the grid the run plans on and not a look-alike.
        # Declared because nothing contributes it: `goal_pose` is a plain ParameterVariationList,
        # which varies a number and knows nothing about placement.
        map: files/depot.yaml
        markers:
        # MAP-frame, unlike the 3D scene's world-frame markers -- this panel *is* the map -- so
        # goal_pose needs no offset here. The robot starts at the map origin: it spawns at world
        # (-8, 0) and map = world + (8, 0), which is also where AMCL is seeded (files/nav2_params.yaml).
        - {kind: pose, pos: [0.0, 0.0], yaw: 0.0, label: start, color: "#60a5fa"}
        - {kind: pose, param: goal_pose, label: goal, color: "#4ade80"}
  results:
    # Web Run view: replay one run over its rosbag timeline. No `scene3d` entry -- the roqsim backend
    # contributes it, because a campaign it runs always records the capture that panel replays.
    run_view:
      panels:
      # Two trees, because they answer different questions: `scenario_tree` is what this trial did,
      # `nav2_behavior_tree` is nav2's internal tree (why the navigator recovered). Corner-anchored to
      # half the column each; both claiming `left` would stack them on top of each other.
      - nav2_behavior_tree:
          position: {anchor: top-left, width: 320, height: "calc(50% - 44px)"}
          minimizable: true
      - scenario_tree:
          title: Scenario
          position: {anchor: bottom-left, width: 320, height: "calc(50% - 44px)"}
          minimizable: true
          source: {table: behaviors}
      - costmap:
          title: Nav2 costmaps
          position: {anchor: top-right, width: 320, height: 550}
          minimizable: true
          layers:
            map: {topic: /map}
            global: {topic: /global_costmap/costmap}
            local: {topic: /local_costmap/costmap}
            poses: {table: poses}
      # Every container's output merged with /rosout on the playback clock. The declared width is what
      # makes `bottom-center` float above the playback bar instead of docking into it.
      - log:
          title: Run log
          position: {anchor: bottom-center, width: "calc(100% - 680px)", height: 380}
          minimizable: true
          minimized: true
      # A `vega` panel and not `timeseries` because speed is derived: the run records poses, not
      # velocities, so it is a finite difference computed in the spec. `datum['position.x']` rather than
      # a `field`, because the CSV column names contain dots.
      #
      # `decimate_hz` because TF outruns the query's 5000-row cap in a couple of minutes, and that cap
      # cuts at the HEAD: without it the chart would stop mid-run while looking finished. It also widens
      # the interval the speed below is differenced over, which costs nothing here -- the raw difference
      # of consecutive TF samples is the noise the rolling mean exists to hide.
      - vega:
          title: base_link
          position: {anchor: bottom-right, width: 320, height: 380}
          minimizable: true
          source: {table: poses, filter: {frame: base_link}, decimate_hz: 5}
          vega_lite:
            resolve: {scale: {x: shared}}
            spacing: 8
            transform:
            - {calculate: "datum['position.x']", as: px}
            - {calculate: "datum['position.y']", as: py}
            - {calculate: "datum['orientation.yaw']", as: yaw}
            - window:
              - {op: lag, field: px, as: px0}
              - {op: lag, field: py, as: py0}
              - {op: lag, field: timestamp, as: t0}
              sort: [{field: timestamp}]
            - filter: "isValid(datum.t0)"
            # Drop zero-length intervals rather than clamping them: two TF samples can share a receive
            # time, and max(dt, 1e-6) would turn such a pair into a 4000 m/s spike.
            - filter: "datum.timestamp - datum.t0 > 0"
            - calculate: >
                sqrt(pow(datum.px - datum.px0, 2) + pow(datum.py - datum.py0, 2))
                / (datum.timestamp - datum.t0)
              as: speed
            - window: [{op: mean, field: speed, as: speed_avg}]
              frame: [-9, 0]
              sort: [{field: timestamp}]
            vconcat:
            - height: 150
              encoding:
                x: {field: timestamp, type: quantitative, axis: null}
                y: {field: speed_avg, type: quantitative, title: "speed [m/s]"}
              layer:
              # The faint raw line keeps visible what the rolling mean smoothed away.
              - mark: {type: line, opacity: 0.25, strokeWidth: 1}
                encoding: {y: {field: speed, type: quantitative}}
              - mark: {type: line, strokeWidth: 1.5}
            - height: 120
              transform: [{fold: [px, py, yaw], as: [series, value]}]
              mark: {type: line, strokeWidth: 1.5}
              encoding:
                x: {field: timestamp, type: quantitative, title: "t [s]"}
                y: {field: value, type: quantitative, title: pose}
                color: {field: series, type: nominal, legend: {title: null, orient: top}}
    # Results-explorer notebook views, one per tree level. The same three the Gazebo half declares, so
    # both backends are read through identical views.
    explorer:
      notebooks:
      - Analysis:
          run: analysis/analysis_run.ipynb
          config: analysis/analysis_config.ipynb
          campaign: analysis/analysis_campaign.ipynb
execution:
  containers:
  # roqsim in a container of its own. `roqsim` is an entry point in the `robovast.simulators`
  # group shipped by RoboVAST's `roqsim` extra, so it resolves wherever the campaign is composed
  # -- including the in-cluster service. Nothing about the simulator travels with the campaign.
    simulation:
      backend: roqsim
  # A path, because roqsim_nav2_example registers no `roqsim.worlds` provider. The backend declares it as
  # a run file, so it is mounted at /config/<path> and archived with the campaign; the world
  # `extends: roqsim_scenes:depot`, which is packaged and travels with the image, so only this YAML
  # moves -- and the backend can see that from the one `extends:` key without opening anything
  # else. A world extending a CAMPAIGN file instead has a chain only the simulator can resolve, so
  # the backend asks `roqsim scenes inputs` in roqsim's own image and stages what it names.
  #
  # This is the campaign-wide DEFAULT. A configuration may resolve its own world through the `sim:`
  # channel; see the `sim` key on a configuration entry, and docs/simulators.rst.
      config: world/depot_nav2.yaml
  # No `image:` here, and that is the point: the roqsim backend supplies the family member it
  # needs, and which registry it comes from is the deployment's business (ROBOVAST_PROJECT), not
  # this file's. Naming a ref here pinned the example to one site's private registry and made it
  # unrunnable for anyone who cloned the repo.
  #
  # To run a whole campaign against another project: `--image-project`, or ROBOVAST_PROJECT.
      resources:
  # Request the sustained figure, cap generously. The simulator is not under test, and its
  # peak-to-mean ratio in this world is ~18 (0.34 cores sustained, 5.98 at the startup burst
  # where depot's geometry compiles) -- so there is no honest single number. Reserving the
  # peak costs more than the un-tuned campaign did; capping at the sustained figure clips a
  # burst that changes nothing the robot experiences, because realtime pacing normalises what
  # the simulated world looks like and runs.clock_map_* records per run whether it kept pace.
        cpu: 0.5
        cpu_limit: 6
        memory: 2944Mi
  # The system under test, kept vanilla: a nav2 image plus the TB4 description and nothing of ours.
  # That is the claim this shape makes -- point it at any nav2 image and it works.
  #
  # It has to name an image: a `sut` is the campaign's own container, and RoboVAST has no default
  # for one -- inventing an image for the thing under test would run something nobody chose. This
  # names the framework image *by family member*, so it still follows ROBOVAST_PROJECT; a concrete
  # ref here would be used verbatim, which is what you want for a real third-party nav2 image.
    sut:
      image: family:robovast
      system_packages:
  # The description nav2_bringup's tb4_simulation_launch.py uses, so both halves publish an
  # identical TF tree. files/nav2_sut.launch.py feeds it to robot_state_publisher.
      - ros-jazzy-nav2-minimal-tb4-description
      resources:
  # NO split here -- but the property being bought is that the ceiling never BINDS, not that
  # the same number is in force everywhere. An allocation the container never reaches cannot
  # have shaped the run; equality is the conservative way to get there while nothing measures
  # what it actually got. (Per-node sizing already varies the figure, so "identical in every
  # run" was never the rule this could enforce.) Sized above its measured peak of 2.91, and
  # run_validity_view says per run whether it held: quota_bound for this ceiling, contended
  # for other work taking cores it had not reserved.
  #
  # What that peak does not cover: it comes from a pilot, and clipping is non-linear -- 0.5%
  # of ticks over the limit cost 22% of runs. A search proposing harder configurations than
  # the pilot can exceed it, so watch quota_bound rather than assuming the headroom holds.
        cpu: 3
        memory: 640Mi
    scenario:
      resources:
  # Same reasoning as the simulator: scenario-execution is not under test. Measured 0.52
  # sustained, 1.23 peak -- the old flat 1 was under its own peak, so it throttled.
        cpu: 0.75
        cpu_limit: 2
        memory: 896Mi
  mode: ros2
  runs: 10
  # Without it `stalled` is null, no verdict about a wedged run is possible, and
  # `vast wait` cannot exit on one -- a campaign that hangs looks like one that is
  # still working. 300 rather than the searches' 600 because this is batch mode: its
  # bag conversion runs after the campaign rather than between batches, so progress
  # never legitimately pauses mid-run. Measured: 50 trials in 373 s at ~25 concurrent,
  # so a run takes ~150 s and this is a backstop with room, not a bound.
  timeout: 300
  scenario_file: scenario_roqsim.osc
  run_files:
  # Per-extension rather than "files/*": that glob crosses '/', so it also swept
  # files/__pycache__/*.pyc -- and run_files are hashed into the config identity, so a stray
  # recompile perturbed it. These reach the SUT too, which is how a vanilla image can be handed
  # nav2_sut.launch.py to run.
  - "files/*.yaml"
  - "files/*.pgm"
  - "files/*.xml"
  - "files/*.sdf"
  - "files/*.py"
  env:
  - PYTHONUNBUFFERED: "1"
results_processing:
  postprocessing:
  # `all` extracts every TF child frame that resolves against map, so a world that later gains a
  # walker shows it without editing this file. `require` names what the analysis is measured from
  # -- base_link, odom (places the local costmap in the map frame) and turtlebot4_base_link_gt
  # (ground truth, shifted +8 m in x in analysis) -- and a run missing any of them fails loudly.
  - rosbags_tf_to_csv:
      frames: all
      require: [base_link, odom, turtlebot4_base_link_gt]
  - rosbags_nav2bt_to_csv
  - nav2_bt_tree: {bt_xml: files/nav2_bt.xml}
  - rosbags_costmap_to_csv:
      topics: [/map, /global_costmap/costmap, /local_costmap/costmap]
